explanation:
setTimeout callbacks are macrotasks, so they only run after all the synchronous code (both loops) has finished. var is function-scoped, not block-scoped, so the first loop's callbacks all share one i, which is already 3 by then; let creates a new i for each iteration, so each callback keeps its own value.

